# Mashal AI Video

Turn Your Ideas Into Amazing Videos — an AI video generation platform. Users write a
prompt (or upload an image / script), choose style and settings, and the app hands the
job to a pluggable AI video provider, tracks it as a real asynchronous job, and saves
the finished video to the user's library.

This is a real, working full-stack application — not a mockup. **No part of the UI
fakes a successful generation.** If no video provider is configured, the app tells the
user exactly that, everywhere it matters, and the rest of the platform (accounts,
dashboard, credits, library) still works normally.

---

## 1. Technology stack

| Layer | Choice |
|---|---|
| Frontend | React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS |
| Backend | Node.js + TypeScript + Express |
| ORM / DB | Prisma — SQLite for local dev, PostgreSQL for production (one env var) |
| Auth | Email/password (bcrypt + signed httpOnly session cookie) + Google OAuth |
| Storage | Storage abstraction (local disk in dev; swap in S3/R2 for prod) |
| Video provider | Pluggable `VideoProvider` interface; ships with a "not configured" No-Op provider and an optional fal.ai provider |

## 2. Project structure

```
mashal-ai-video/
  backend/          Express API, Prisma schema, provider abstraction, jobs
  frontend/          React app (landing, auth, dashboard, studio, library, admin)
```

## 3. Installation

Requires Node.js 18+.

```bash
# Backend
cd backend
npm install
cp .env.example .env      # fill in values, see section 5
npx prisma migrate dev --name init
npm run dev                # http://localhost:4000

# Frontend (in a second terminal)
cd frontend
npm install
cp .env.example .env
npm run dev                # http://localhost:5173
```

The frontend dev server proxies `/api` to the backend — no CORS config needed locally.

## 4. Database setup

The schema's datasource is PostgreSQL (see `backend/prisma/schema.prisma`), and the
initial migration is already committed at
`backend/prisma/migrations/20260831000000_init/`, covering every model
(`User`, `Video`, `GenerationJob`, `CreditTransaction`, `Subscription`, `Payment`).

- **Production**: set `DATABASE_URL` to your Postgres connection string and run
  `npx prisma migrate deploy` (the backend's `npm start` already does this
  automatically on every boot — see section 11).
- **Local dev without a Postgres instance**: either point `DATABASE_URL` at a local
  or free-tier Postgres (e.g. one from Render, Neon, or Supabase) and run
  `npx prisma migrate deploy`, or temporarily switch the datasource `provider` in
  `schema.prisma` back to `sqlite`, set `DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"`, and run
  `npx prisma migrate dev --name init` (don't commit that provider change).

## 5. Environment variables

See `backend/.env.example` and `frontend/.env.example` for the full list. Never commit
a real `.env` file — both are already in `.gitignore`.

Key backend variables:

- `DATABASE_URL` — SQLite file path in dev, Postgres URL in prod.
- `SESSION_SECRET` — long random string used to sign session cookies. Generate with
  `openssl rand -base64 48`.
- `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` / `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` — from the Google Cloud Console, only
  needed once you enable Google sign-in.
- `FAL_API_KEY` — **this is where you add your AI video provider's API key.** Leave it
  unset and the app runs fine with video generation showing as "not configured."
- `STORAGE_*` — object storage credentials for videos/thumbnails/uploads. Local dev
  falls back to disk storage under `backend/uploads/` if these are unset.
- `PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY` — set this once you wire up Stripe or another provider (see
  section 8). Left unset, the pricing page still renders; checkout is disabled.

## 6. Authentication setup

- Email/password auth is fully implemented: registration, login, logout,
  forgot/reset password, and session cookies (httpOnly, signed, `SameSite=Lax`).
- Google sign-in uses the standard OAuth 2.0 authorization-code flow. To enable it:
  create OAuth credentials in Google Cloud Console, set the redirect URI to
  `{API_URL}/api/auth/google/callback`, and put the client ID/secret in `.env`.
  Until you do this, the "Continue with Google" button shows a clear
  "Google sign-in is not configured" message instead of failing silently.

## 7. Adding a real AI video provider

The app talks to video providers only through `backend/src/providers/VideoProvider.ts`:

```ts
interface VideoProvider {
  generateVideo(job: GenerationRequest): Promise<{ providerJobId: string }>;
  getGenerationStatus(providerJobId: string): Promise<ProviderStatus>;
  cancelGeneration(providerJobId: string): Promise<void>;
  getResult(providerJobId: string): Promise<{ videoUrl: string; thumbnailUrl?: string }>;
}
```

- `providers/NoOpProvider.ts` is the default — it's what makes the app correctly say
  "Video provider is not configured yet" instead of faking a result.
- `providers/FalProvider.ts` is a real, isolated starting point for fal.ai. **Before
  using it in production, check fal.ai's current API docs** for the exact
  model/endpoint you want (text-to-video vs image-to-video models change), and fill in
  the request/response mapping — the shape of that mapping is intentionally left
  explicit rather than guessed, so you don't ship against invented endpoints.
- Set `FAL_API_KEY` and `VIDEO_PROVIDER=fal` in `.env` to activate it. The key never
  reaches the frontend — all provider calls happen server-side.
- To add another provider, implement the same interface and register it in
  `providers/index.ts`.

## 8. Adding a payment provider

`Payment` and `Subscription` Prisma models, plus webhook-ready routes, are in place,
but no payment processor is wired up — the spec explicitly asks not to fake payment
confirmation or build custom card handling. To add Stripe:

1. `npm install stripe` in `backend/`.
2. Create `backend/src/services/paymentService.ts` implementing checkout-session
   creation and webhook signature verification, using `PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY`.
3. Only mark a `Payment` row `succeeded` inside the verified webhook handler — never
   from a client-side call.

## 9. Admin setup

Set a user's `role` to `admin` directly in the database (or via
`npx prisma studio`) to grant access to `/admin`. Every admin API route re-checks the
role server-side — hiding the nav link is not the authorization mechanism.

## 10. Running locally

```bash
cd backend && npm run dev     # API on :4000
cd frontend && npm run dev    # app on :5173
```

## 11. Production deployment

This repo is configured to deploy as **one service**: the Express backend also serves
the built frontend (see the static-file block near the end of `backend/src/index.ts`).
That keeps everything on a single origin, which matters for auth — cookies stay
first-party, including on Safari/iOS, where cross-site cookies are blocked by default.

- `render.yaml` at the repo root is a [Render Blueprint](https://render.com/docs/blueprint-spec)
  that provisions this web service plus a managed Postgres database automatically —
  see section 12 for the deploy walkthrough (including from a phone, no CLI needed).
- The build runs `frontend`'s `vite build` then `backend`'s `tsc` build; the start
  command runs `prisma migrate deploy` (applying the committed migration) and then
  starts the server.
- Set every secret in section 5 in your host's environment variable manager — never in
  the repo. `render.yaml` marks each one `sync: false` so Render prompts for it.
- The video provider, Google sign-in, and payments stay in their "not configured"
  state until you add their credentials — nothing here is required to deploy.
- Local disk storage for uploads (the dev fallback) does **not** persist across
  deploys or restarts on most hosts, including Render. Put real object storage
  credentials in `STORAGE_*` before you rely on uploads/videos surviving a redeploy.
- Deploying frontend and backend as two separate services (e.g. frontend on a static
  host, backend elsewhere) is also possible — set `VITE_API_URL` to the backend's URL
  and `APP_URL`/CORS accordingly — but then cross-site cookie behavior on Safari/iOS
  needs its own handling, which the single-service setup above avoids entirely.

## 12. Deploying from an iPhone (GitHub → Render, no computer needed)

This needs the GitHub app and Safari or Chrome on the iPhone — no terminal, no SSH,
no desktop at any point.

1. **Get the code onto GitHub.**
   - If you haven't already, create a new repository at github.com/new (the mobile
     web site works fine for this) and upload this project to it. The simplest path
     from a phone: install the **GitHub** app, or use github.com's "Add file → Upload
     files" in Safari/Chrome to upload the whole `mashal-ai-video` folder.
   - Make sure `.env` files are **not** among the uploaded files — only `.env.example`
     should be there. (`.gitignore` already excludes `.env`/`.env.local` if you're
     pushing via git rather than a manual upload.)
2. **Create a Render account.** Go to render.com in Safari, sign up (GitHub sign-in is
   the fastest — no credit card required for the free tier).
3. **Connect the repo.** In the Render dashboard: **New → Blueprint**. Authorize
   Render to access your GitHub account, then pick the `mashal-ai-video` repository.
   Render finds `render.yaml` at the repo root automatically and shows you the
   web service + Postgres database it's about to create.
4. **Fill in the prompted values.** Render will ask for each `sync: false` variable
   from `render.yaml`. At minimum, leave `APP_URL` and `API_URL` blank for now — you'll
   fill those in after step 5 once you know the live URL. Everything else (video
   provider, Google OAuth, storage, payments) can stay blank; the app runs fine
   without them.
5. **Deploy.** Tap **Apply** / **Create Blueprint**. Render builds and deploys — this
   takes a few minutes the first time. When it finishes, Render shows your live URL,
   something like `https://mashal-ai-video.onrender.com`.
6. **Set `APP_URL` and `API_URL`.** Go to the web service → **Environment**, set both
   variables to that exact URL (e.g. `https://mashal-ai-video.onrender.com`, no
   trailing slash), and save — Render redeploys automatically with the new values.
7. **Visit the URL.** The site should load, and sign-up/login/studio/dashboard should
   all work immediately (video generation will correctly say "not configured" until
   you add a real provider — see section 7).

Notes for the free tier: the web service spins down after 15 minutes idle (the next
visit takes 30–60 seconds to wake up), and the free Postgres database expires 30 days
after creation with a 14-day grace period — export or upgrade to a paid Postgres plan
before then if you want to keep the data. Both are one-tap upgrades from the Render
dashboard whenever you're ready, from the same phone.

## 13. What's intentionally NOT included yet

Per the "no fake functionality" requirement, these are scaffolded but not
activated, since activating them requires your own credentials:

- A live video provider call (ships as "not configured" until you add `FAL_API_KEY`).
- Google sign-in (until you add OAuth credentials).
- Real payment processing (until you wire a processor into `paymentService.ts`).
- Production object storage (falls back to local disk in dev).

Everything else — accounts, sessions, the full video studio UI, async job tracking,
server-side credits, the video library, and the admin panel — is real and working.
